menuconfig CAM_MOTOR_DW9807
    bool "DW9807 VCM (Raspberry Pi Camera Module 3 autofocus)"
    default n
    select CAMERA_MOTOR_DEVICE_USED
    help
        Enable the Dongwoon DW9807 voice-coil-motor driver - the autofocus
        actuator fitted to the Raspberry Pi Camera Module 3 / NoIR 3, at I2C
        0x0c on the same bus as the IMX708.

        Without this the lens never moves: it sits wherever its spring rests it,
        which is past infinity, and every picture is soft no matter how well the
        exposure and colour are tuned.

        Closed-loop autofocus additionally needs ESP_IPA_AF_ALGORITHM,
        ESP_VIDEO_ENABLE_CAMERA_MOTOR_CONTROLLER and
        ESP_VIDEO_ISP_PIPELINE_CONTROL_CAMERA_MOTOR, plus an "af" block in the
        sensor's IPA tuning JSON. With the motor alone you get a lens you can
        position by hand through V4L2_CID_FOCUS_ABSOLUTE, but nothing that
        decides where to put it.

if CAM_MOTOR_DW9807

    config CAM_MOTOR_DW9807_AUTO_DETECT
        bool "Auto-detect the DW9807"
        default y
        help
            Register the DW9807 in the esp_cam_sensor motor auto-detect list so
            esp_video probes for it during esp_video_init(). The application
            still has to pass a cam_motor entry and ESP_VIDEO_INIT_FLAGS_MOTOR
            in its esp_video_init_config_t - auto-detect only decides which
            drivers are candidates, not whether probing happens at all.

    config CAM_MOTOR_DW9807_INIT_POS
        int "Lens park position at start-up (DAC code)"
        range 0 1023
        default 512
        help
            Where the lens is placed as soon as the motor is detected, before
            the autofocus loop has converged - so it governs how the first few
            frames look, and it is the whole answer for a build with no AF
            algorithm.

            The DAC is 10-bit, but only part of that travel is useful. Measured
            on this module with the focus sweep in examples/imx708_snapshot:
            codes below ~384 are a dead plateau with the lens against its stop,
            the rise starts at 448, and a ~2 m subject peaked at 576.

            The default of 512 sits just above the dead zone, so it is sharp for
            distant subjects without wasting the park on travel that does
            nothing. It was 450 - that turned out to be at the very bottom of
            the rise and measurably soft (1467 against 1837 at the peak). Raise
            it towards 700-900 if the camera mostly looks at things within
            arm's reach.

            Re-measure on your own module: VCM assemblies vary unit to unit, and
            the figures above are from one specific Camera Module 3.

    config CAM_MOTOR_DW9807_PERIOD_US
        int "Settling time allowed per DAC code (microseconds)"
        range 1 65535
        default 3000
        help
            Reported to the autofocus algorithm as step_period.period_in_us, and
            used together with the timestamp of the last move to decide when the
            ISP's focus statistics for a new lens position can be believed.

            The DW9807 applies a new code immediately; what takes time is the
            lens settling on its spring, which is closer to a fixed ring-down
            than to something proportional to distance travelled - so this is a
            linear approximation of a non-linear thing. The default gives a
            coarse scan step (tens of codes) about a frame to settle.

            Raised from 1000 to 3000 after the sweep: at 1000 a coarse scan step
            of ~50 codes got only 50 ms, but a frame is 36 ms at 28 fps with two
            buffers in flight, so the real move-to-statistics-reflect-it latency
            is nearer 70-110 ms plus mechanical settle. The sweep allowed five
            frames per position and produced a clean single-peaked curve, so
            that much patience is known to be enough; 3000 gives a coarse step
            about the same budget.

            Raise it further if autofocus lands on a different position each
            time it runs on the same static scene: that is the signature of
            definition being sampled while the lens is still moving.

endif # CAM_MOTOR_DW9807
